  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-sparse-hamming-packing": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d,k$ be natural numbers with $1\\le k\\le d/2$. Let $m$ and $r$ be the\nnatural quotients determined by\n$$mk\\le d<(m+1)k,\\qquad 4r\\le k<4(r+1).$$\nThus $m\\ge2$. Write $[m]=\\{0,1,\\ldots,m-1\\}$ and give $[m]^k$ the Hamming\ndistance $d_H(\\omega,\\omega')=\\#\\{j<k:\\omega_j\\ne\\omega'_j\\}$. There is a\nset $\\mathcal C\\subseteq[m]^k$ such that distinct $\\omega,\\omega'\\in\\mathcal C$\nsatisfy $d_H(\\omega,\\omega')>r$ and\n$$\\log|\\mathcal C|\\ge \\frac{k}{1024}\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{ed}{k}\\right).$$\nFor each $\\omega\\in\\mathcal C$, place one $1$ in each of the first $k$ disjoint\ncoordinate blocks of size $m$, at position $\\omega_j$ in block $j$, and put\n$0$ in all remaining coordinates of $\\mathbb R^d$. The resulting vectors\n$u^\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^d$ have exactly $k$ nonzero coordinates and satisfy\n$$\\|u^\\omega-u^{\\omega'}\\|_2^2>k/2\\qquad(\\omega\\ne\\omega').$$"
    ],
    "def-kullback-leibler-divergence": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $P,Q$ be probability measures on the same measurable space\n([[def-probability-measure]]). Absolute continuity $P\\ll Q$ has the convention\nof [[def-absolutely-continuous-with-respect-to-a-positive-measure]]. If\n$P\\not\\ll Q$, define\n$D(P\\|Q)=+\\infty$. If $P\\ll Q$, assume AC ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) as in\n[[def-likelihood-ratio-as-rn-derivative]] and let $r=dP/dQ$ be a nonnegative\nmeasurable representative of its Radon–Nikodym class. On $\\{r=0\\}$ set\n$\\ell=0$, and on $\\{r>0\\}$ set $\\ell=\\log r$. Since\n$P(\\{r=0\\})=\\int_{\\{r=0\\}}r\\,dQ=0$, the choice on this set does not affect\n$\\int\\ell\\,dP$. Define\n$$D(P\\|Q):=\\int \\ell\\,dP,$$\nwhere $\\log$ is the natural logarithm ([[def-natural-logarithm]]).\n\nThe definition is independent of the representative: two representatives\nagree $Q$-almost everywhere, hence $P$-almost everywhere. By the\nchange-of-measure identity of [[thm-likelihood-ratio-change-of-measure]],\nthis is equivalently\n$$D(P\\|Q)=\\int r\\log r\\,dQ,$$\nwhere $0\\log0:=0$. Interpret the signed integral by the difference of the\nnonnegative integrals of its positive and negative parts\n([[def-positive-and-negative-parts-of-a-function]],\n[[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]]). The negative part is finite: for\n$0<t\\le1$, the derivative of $-t\\log t$ is $-\\log t-1$, so this function\nhas maximum $1/e$ at $t=1/e$; here $e$ is the natural exponential constant\n([[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]]) and the logarithm derivative is\n[[thm-integral-logarithm-derivative-and-normalisation]]. Thus\nthe $Q$-integral of the negative part of $r\\log r$ is at most $1/e$, and the\nextended integral is well-defined without\nan $\\infty-\\infty$ ambiguity.\n\nThis convention is directional: the first argument must be absolutely\ncontinuous with respect to the second for the integral formula to apply."
    ],
    "def-mutual-information-for-finite-hypothesis-testing": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $M\\ge2$ be an integer, let $P_1,\\ldots,P_M$ be probability measures on\none measurable sample space, and let $J$ be uniform on $[M]=\\{1,\\ldots,M\\}$.\nConditional on $J=j$, let the observation $X$ have law $P_j$. Define the\nmixture law\n$$\\bar P:=\\frac1M\\sum_{j=1}^M P_j$$\nand the **mutual information** of this finite experiment by\n$$I(J;X):=\\frac1M\\sum_{j=1}^M D(P_j\\|\\bar P).$$\nHere $D$ is the directional KL divergence of\n[[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]], and logarithms are natural. Each\n$P_j\\ll\\bar P$, since $\\bar P(A)=0$ forces $P_j(A)=0$ for every $j$. Moreover,\n$dP_j/d\\bar P\\le M$ almost everywhere, because $\\bar P\\ge P_j/M$ as measures;\nthe negative part of $t\\log t$ is bounded. Thus every term in the finite sum\nis well-defined and finite.\n\nThe uniform prior is part of this definition. A nonuniform prior requires its\nown weights in the mixture and in the KL average."
    ],
    "thm-pinsker": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. For probability measures $P,Q$ on one measurable space, with\n$$\\|P-Q\\|_{\\rm TV}:=\\sup_{A}|P(A)-Q(A)|,$$\nthe event-supremum convention without a factor $1/2$,\n$$\\|P-Q\\|_{\\rm TV}\\le\\sqrt{\\frac12D(P\\|Q)}.$$\nIf $P\\not\\ll Q$, the right side is $+\\infty$ by the definition of KL\ndivergence."
    ],
    "thm-product-kl-additivity": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Let $n\\ge1$ be finite. For each $i\\le n$, let\n$(X_i,\\mathcal A_i)$ be a measurable space and let $P_i,Q_i$ be probability\nmeasures on it. On the finite product measurable space set\n$$P=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^nP_i,\\qquad Q=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^nQ_i.$$\nWith the KL convention of [[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]],\n$$D(P\\|Q)=\\sum_{i=1}^nD(P_i\\|Q_i),$$\nwhere a sum with an infinite term is $+\\infty$."
    ],
    "lem-gaussian-location-kl-formula": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC and let $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\tau>0$. For the normal location\nlaws with common variance $\\tau^2$,\n$$D\\bigl(N(a,\\tau^2)\\|N(b,\\tau^2)\\bigr)=\\frac{(a-b)^2}{2\\tau^2}.$$"
    ],
    "thm-two-point-le-cam-risk-bound": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet an experiment have parameter set $\\{0,1\\}$, sample space $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$,\nand laws $P_0,P_1$. Let the scalar targets satisfy $t_0<t_1$ and put\n$\\Delta=t_1-t_0>0$. An arbitrary randomized estimator is a probability\nkernel $K$ from $\\mathcal X$ to $\\mathbb R$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and\nits loss at parameter $j$ is squared error $(a-t_j)^2$. For every such $K$,\n$$\\max_{j\\in\\{0,1\\}} R_j(K)\\ge \\frac{\\Delta^2}{8}\\bigl(1-\\|P_0-P_1\\|_{\\rm TV}\\bigr),$$\nwhere $\\|P_0-P_1\\|_{\\rm TV}=\\sup_{A\\in\\mathcal A}|P_0(A)-P_1(A)|$.\nConsequently the minimax risk over all randomized estimators obeys the same\nlower bound."
    ],
    "thm-assouad-hypercube": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d\\ge1$, $a>0$, and consider the finite experiment indexed by\n$\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^d$ with sample laws $P_\\omega$. The target is\n$t_\\omega=a\\omega\\in\\mathbb R^d$, the action is in $\\mathbb R^d$, and loss\nis squared Euclidean distance. For each coordinate $i$, let $\\omega^{(i)}$\ndenote the vector with bit $i$ flipped, and set\n$$v:=\\max_{\\omega\\in\\{0,1\\}^d,\\ 1\\le i\\le d} \\|P_\\omega-P_{\\omega^{(i)}}\\|_{\\rm TV}.$$\nFor every randomized estimator kernel $K$,\n$$\\max_{\\omega}R_\\omega(K)\\ge\\frac{a^2d}{8}(1-v),$$\nand the same lower bound holds for the minimax risk. The total variation\nconvention is $\\|P-Q\\|_{\\rm TV}=\\sup_A|P(A)-Q(A)|$."
    ],
    "thm-fano-packing": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $M\\ge2$ be finite, let $P_1,\\ldots,P_M$ be sample laws on one\nmeasurable space, and let $t_1,\\ldots,t_M$ lie in a metric space\n$(\\mathcal T,\\rho)$ with its Borel sigma-algebra. Suppose\n$\\rho(t_j,t_l)\\ge\\Delta>0$ whenever $j\\ne l$. Let $J$ be uniform on\n$[M]=\\{1,\\ldots,M\\}$, with conditional observation law $P_j$ given $J=j$.\nFor any probability reference law $Q$, define\n$$K_Q:=\\frac1M\\sum_{j=1}^M D(P_j\\|Q)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor every randomized estimator kernel from the observation to $\\mathcal T$,\nwith squared metric loss $\\rho(a,t_j)^2$, its uniform average risk and its\nmaximum risk are each at least\n$$\\frac{\\Delta^2}{4} \\left(1-\\frac{K_Q+\\log2}{\\log M}\\right).$$\nThe minimax risk over all randomized estimators obeys this bound as well.\nWhen $K_Q=+\\infty$, the displayed lower bound is interpreted as $-\\infty$\nand is vacuous. Logarithms are natural."
    ],
    "ex-gaussian-two-point-le-cam-lower-bound": [],
    "ex-sparse-gaussian-sequence-fano-lower-bound": [],
    "ex-gaussian-hypercube-assouad-lower-bound": []
  }
